 ENDF/B-VIII.0 EVAL-Oct16 Brown,Nobre,Herman,Leal,Trkov,+         2634 1451   21
 Original ENDF/B-VIII.0 evaluation from the CIELO project         2634 1451   22
 The evaluation, file assembly and criticality validation         2634 1451   23
 were performed at BNL and IAEA by                                2634 1451   24
 D.A. Brown, G.P.A. Nobre, M. Herman, R. Arcilla, L.Leal,         2634 1451   25
 R. Capote, A. Trkov                                              2634 1451   26
                                                                  2634 1451   27
 Primary reference: Nucl. Data Sheets 148, 214 (2018)             2634 1451   28
 The evaluation was performed up to 150 MeV for 7 subsequent      2634 1451   29
 neutron emissions, 3 proton emissions and a single alpha-        2634 1451   30
 particle, deuteron, triton, and 3He for a total of 146           2634 1451   31
 residual nuclei. Only 5 of these were treated as exclusive,      2634 1451   32
 i.e., had an explicit reaction assigned to them along with the   2634 1451   33
 corresponding exclusive spectra for all particles and gammas.    2634 1451   34
 The exclusive reactions were (n,g), (n,elas), (n,inel), (n,      2634 1451   35
 2n), (n,p), and (n, 2p). For all the remaining we provide        2634 1451   36
 cross sections for residua production which represent a sum of   2634 1451   37
 contributions from all possible reactions populating a given     2634 1451   38
 residue. Their respective spectra are lumped together            2634 1451   39
 providing only seven cumulative spectra (for gammas, neutrons,   2634 1451   40
 protons, alphas, deuterons, tritons, and 3He) for all the        2634 1451   41
 inclusive channels.                                              2634 1451   42
                                                                  2634 1451   43
 Development of the evaluation is traced in the                   2634 1451   44
 CIELO-iron project on the NNDC GForge server:                    2634 1451   45
 https://ndclx4.bnl.gov/gf/project/cielo_iron                     2634 1451   46
 All elements of the evaluation, such as experimental datasets,   2634 1451   47
 EMPIRE inputs and outputs, intermediate ENDF files and plots     2634 1451   48
 can be followed there in a chronological order.                  2634 1451   49
                                                                  2634 1451   50
 EXPERIMENTAL DATABASE                                            2634 1451   51
 --------------------                                             2634 1451   52
 The experimental database used in the evaluation is summarized   2634 1451   53
 in Table IX of the primary reference (NDS 148,241(2018)). In     2634 1451   54
 doing this selection we favored: (i) broad energy range covered  2634 1451   55
 by the experiment, (ii) high energy resolution, and (iii) high   2634 1451   56
 (but reasonable) accuracy of the experiment. Thus the            2634 1451   57
 measurements providing possibly wide and reliable picture were   2634 1451   58
 given precedence over single energy-point experiments,           2634 1451   59
 especially within the energy range where strong fluctuations     2634 1451   60
 were observed. On the other hand, many single- or a few-point    2634 1451   61
 measurements adopted when no extended datasets were              2634 1451   62
 available. Some of the measured datasets were renormalized to    2634 1451   63
 account for changes in the reference cross sections since they   2634 1451   64
 were used in the analysis of the experiments. The                2634 1451   65
 renormalizations built into the EXFOR retrieval system were      2634 1451   66
 adopted. In case of the (n,p) and (n,2n) reactions we took       2634 1451   67
 advantage of the existing high quality evaluations available     2634 1451   68
 from the IRDFF library (INDC(NDS)-0616, IAEA, Vienna (2012))     2634 1451   69
 and used these data instead of the actual measurements.          2634 1451   70
                                                                  2634 1451   71
 RESOLVED RESONANCE RANGE                                         2634 1451   72
 ------------------------                                         2634 1451   73
 MF=2                                                             2634 1451   74
   MT=151 Resolved resonances                                     2634 1451   75
     The evaluation in the resonance region in based on the       2634 1451   76
     version of the Atlas that was still in development at the    2634 1451   77
     time our work was performed [3].  The compilation in the     2634 1451   78
     Atlas appeared to use parameters generated in Reich-Moore    2634 1451   79
     approximation. Because the Atlas also contained resonance    2634 1451   80
     parameters for resonances in the first (n,n) state, we       2634 1451   81
     decided to convert the Atlas compilation to the LRF=7        2634 1451   82
     format using Reich-Moore approximation, including the        2634 1451   83
     first inelastic state.  The change from MLBW Atlas           2634 1451   84
     parameters to LRF=7 parameters necessitated a refitting      2634 1451   85
     of the bound levels and R-prime. This change did improve     2634 1451   86
     agreement between the evaluation and experiment in the       2634 1451   87
     minima between resonances. The capture widths were known     2634 1451   88
     only from the area under each resonance. Additionally we     2634 1451   89
     added several resonances at the upper end of the RRR to      2634 1451   90
     improve agreement of the RRR with data in the region of      2634 1451   91
     140-165 keV.  The (n,n) resonances were determined by        2634 1451   92
     identifying capture resonances with no elastic               2634 1451   93
     counterpart and subtracting the capture width from the       2634 1451   94
     total width determined from (n,tot) measurements. The        2634 1451   95
     (n,n) resonances were assumed to be s-wave only.  This       2634 1451   96
     choice was supported by the experimental results of Ref.     2634 1451   97
     [1] and Ref. [2].                                            2634 1451   98
     Our thermal cross section and computed resonance integral    2634 1451   99
     values are in excellent agreement with the Atlas             2634 1451  100
     recommendations [3], as shown in the table below.  Our       2634 1451  101
     MACS(30 keV) value lies roughly in between the JENDL-4.0     2634 1451  102
     and KADoNiS-0.3 values and is generally consistent with      2634 1451  103
     the experimental averaged value given in the new Atlas.      2634 1451  104
     The Atlas value derived from systematics is more             2634 1451  105
     consistent with the KADoNiS-0.3 value. We note that the      2634 1451  106
     in-development version of KADoNiS-1.0 recommends a value     2634 1451  107
     basically taken from the Giubrone thesis [4], measured       2634 1451  108
     at CERN n-TOF. This measurement does not span the whole      2634 1451  109
     energy range of the resonance region. Therefore Giubrone     2634 1451  110
     omits several low energy resonances that would have          2634 1451  111
     provided a smooth background to the 30 keV region,           2634 1451  112
     lowering the MACS(30 keV) value obtained from the            2634 1451  113
     consideration of that set alone. Thus we use the MACS        2634 1451  114
     data for validation purposes only.                           2634 1451  115
                                                                  2634 1451  116
     Calculated 2200-m/s cross sections and res. integrals        2634 1451  117
             thermal   res. integ.    MACS (30 keV)               2634 1451  118
    total    3.087 b   94.48 b                                    2634 1451  119
    elastic  0.604 b   82.25 b                                    2634 1451  120
    capture  2.484 b    1.51 b       35.98 mb                     2634 1451  121
                                                                  2634 1451  122
 REACTION MODELING IN FAST NEUTRON RANGE                          2634 1451  123
 ---------------------------------------                          2634 1451  124
 The fast region calculations were performed using the EMPIRE     2634 1451  125
 code, which is a comprehensive package that integrates into a    2634 1451  126
 single system a number of important physical modules and         2634 1451  127
 evaluation tools. We have adopted coupled channel (CC)           2634 1451  128
 calculations in the incident channel and for the direct          2634 1451  129
 inelastic scattering to collective levels. In addition, we       2634 1451  130
 have allowed for the Distorted Wave Born Approximation (DWBA)    2634 1451  131
 to populate presumably collective levels which are not           2634 1451  132
 included in the ground state band used in the CC calculations    2634 1451  133
 to adjust neutron emission spectra in the high energy range.     2634 1451  134
 For the preequilibrium (PE) emission employed the classical      2634 1451  135
 exciton model (PCROSS module) because of its higher              2634 1451  136
 flexibility in modeling neutron spectra compared with the        2634 1451  137
 physically more advanced, but also more rigid quantum            2634 1451  138
 mechanical models. The same module was also used for the PE      2634 1451  139
 emission of protons and gammas as well as for PE emission of     2634 1451  140
 clusters, the latter taking advantage of the Iwamoto-Harada      2634 1451  141
 model.                                                           2634 1451  142
 The Hauser-Feshbach formulation of the statistical model was     2634 1451  143
 employed to follow sequential emission of particles from the     2634 1451  144
 compound nucleus (CN). The elastic and inelastic angular         2634 1451  145
 distributions of neutrons emitted from the CN were calculated    2634 1451  146
 using the Blatt-Biedenharn formalism. The width fluctuation      2634 1451  147
 factor was accounted for within HRTW formalism applied up to     2634 1451  148
 12.0 MeV incident neutrons. Compound nucleus and direct cross    2634 1451  149
 sections were added incoherently.                                2634 1451  150
 Gilbert-Cameron model for level-density has been adopted         2634 1451  151
 for all nuclei involved in this evaluation. The parameters were  2634 1451  152
 fitted to reproduce the asymptotic behavior of discrete          2634 1451  153
 levels, as well as to correctly describe the observed cross      2634 1451  154
 sections.                                                        2634 1451  155
 Complete g-ray cascades for every excited nucleus were           2634 1451  156
 calculated using M1, E1 and E2 electromagnetic transitions.      2634 1451  157
 g-strength functions for E1 transitions from the RIPL3 MLO1      2634 1451  158
 formulation by Pluyko.                                           2634 1451  159
 Occasionally, we resorted to energy-dependent parameter          2634 1451  160
 scaling or tuning factors, available in the EMPIRE code, to      2634 1451  161
 account for model deficiencies. These corrections were           2634 1451  162
 typically on the level of 1-2% or less and can be found in the   2634 1451  163
 input files. Whenever possible the reaction model parameters     2634 1451  164
 used in the 57Fe evaluation were kept consistent with those      2634 1451  165
 used in the 56Fe evaluation.                                     2634 1451  166
                                                                  2634 1451  167
 FAST NEUTRON RANGE                                               2634 1451  168
 ------------------                                               2634 1451  169
 MF=3 Neutron cross sections                                      2634 1451  170
   EMPIRE calculations adjusted to reproduce experimental data    2634 1451  171
   were adopted unless stated differently below.                  2634 1451  172
                                                                  2634 1451  173
  MT=1 Total                                                      2634 1451  174
    The fluctuations provided in the ENDF/B-VII.1 were            2634 1451  175
    derived from the abundance-weighted 56Fe(n,total) cross       2634 1451  176
    section. To  restore  the fluctuations, we performed a        2634 1451  177
    generalized least-square linear spline fit to the data of     2634 1451  178
    Harvey [5] and that of Pandey [6] in the region from          2634 1451  179
    190 keV to 8 MeV. Both sets were in excellent agreement,      2634 1451  180
    differing only slightly in their treatment of the neutron     2634 1451  181
    time-of-flight. At the energy resolution of our spline        2634 1451  182
    fit, this difference was not observable. The fluctuations     2634 1451  183
    were distributed to the 57Fe(n,elas) and 57Fe(n,g) cross      2634 1451  184
    sections such that both the energy average elastic and        2634 1451  185
    capture cross sections were preserved and so that they        2634 1451  186
    sum to the fluctuating total cross section. The data of       2634 1451  187
    Hibden et al. [7] were disregarded as the target was an       2634 1451  188
    isotopically-enriched iron oxide target and the target        2634 1451  189
    composition was not given to enough precision to extract      2634 1451  190
    meaningful data.                                              2634 1451  191
                                                                  2634 1451  192
  MT=2 Elastic scattering                                         2634 1451  193
    This evaluation is distinctly different from ENDF/B-VII.1     2634 1451  194
    Apart from the lower thermal region, the new evaluation       2634 1451  195
    adopts smooth EMPIRE calculations above the resonance region, 2634 1451  196
    contrary to ENDF/B-VII.1 which propagated 56Fe fluctuations   2634 1451  197
    up to several MeV.                                            2634 1451  198
                                                                  2634 1451  199
  MT=4, 51-91 Inelastic scattering                                2634 1451  200
    57Fe is a special case since the inelastic threshold lies     2634 1451  201
    inside the resonance region requiring the Reich-Moore         2634 1451  202
    formalism and the ENDF-6 option LRF=7, as described in the    2634 1451  203
    resonance section.                                            2634 1451  204
                                                                  2634 1451  205
  MT=5 (n,anything)                                               2634 1451  206
    Sum of all reactions not given explicitly in another MT       2634 1451  207
    number as calculated by the EMPIRE code. This MT includes     2634 1451  208
    majority of the reaction channels - namely all those for which2634 1451  209
    we do not provide a complete set of exclusive emission        2634 1451  210
    spectra. The reason for this unusual representation is a      2634 1451  211
    difficulty to disentangle contributions of different reactions2634 1451  212
    to the gamma spectrum from the final residue. This becomes an 2634 1451  213
    issue at higher incident energies when multiple reactions     2634 1451  214
    contribute to the production of a given residue. Note that    2634 1451  215
    exclusive gamma emission spectrum is required by the ENDF-6   2634 1451  216
    format for each reaction that is treated as exclusive. In the 2634 1451  217
    MT=5 representation, the cross sections can be obtained by    2634 1451  218
    multiplying cumulative cross section given in MT=5 by the     2634 1451  219
    relative yields for production of the residual nuclei given in2634 1451  220
    the subsections of MF/MT=6/5. Particle and gamma emission     2634 1451  221
    spectra are summed up over the MT=5 reactions into the        2634 1451  222
    inclusive spectra given in the respective subsections of MF/  2634 1451  223
                                                                  2634 1451  224
  MT=16 (n,2n)                                                    2634 1451  225
    EMPIRE calculations adjusted to reproduce experimental data.  2634 1451  226
                                                                  2634 1451  227
